d to make all  possible colors we call the quark  charges red green and blue  the strong force makes different colors  attract and light colors repel  of course parts don't really have color  in the usual sense color is just a term  we use to describe which quarks attract  or repel each other  a proton or neutron is made of three  quarks one of each color  so each proton is held together by the  strong force that binds its quarks to  each other  but if you look at two different protons  the attractive force of the different  colors cancels the repulsive force of  the light colors so the strong force  between two protons mostly cancels out  when the universe was above a trillion  degrees the quarks were all moving with  too much energy to stick together  imagine taking two small rocks and  putting sticky tape all over them if you  press them against each other and put  them down they'll stick  but if you throw a third tape covered  rock at them with all your might not  only will it not stick to them it will  knock the first two apart  for the first hundred thousandth of a  second quarks were constantly colliding  so forcefully that they bounced off each  other despite the tons of force pulling  the differently colored ones together  even if two quarks that happen to have  less energy than average were able to  stick to each other another quark would  immediately hit them so hard that they  would be knocked apart again  in the very rapidly expanding early  universe though the density and  temperature dropped quickly  that means that over time the quarks  were moving on average more and more  slowly  at a hundred thousandth of a second when  the temperature dropped below a trillion  degrees the quarks were no longer moving  fast enough to resist the forces pulling  them together  each quark almost instantly found quarks  of the two other colors combining into  color-balanced triads of red green blue  those triads were protons and neutrons  which is the form most quarks in the  universe have been in ever since  quarks combining into protons and  neutrons was the universe's first  example of a process that i talked about  in the first lecture  as the universe cooled particles  combined into ever-larger structures  but after the formation of protons and  neutrons the next big change wasn't  about particles combining but instead  about particles being destroyed  as i mentioned in the first lecture most  particles have corresponding  antiparticles with the same mass but  opposite charge  for example electrons have negative  charge and their antiparticles called  positrons have positive charge  collectively these anti-particles are  called antimatter  we don't see naturally occurring  antimatter around us that's because when  a particle collides with its own  antiparticle the two can annihilate and  release their energy usually in the form  of electromagnetic radiation  in the reverse process if you  concentrate high energy radiation in a  small enough region the energy of that  radiation can create a particle  antiparticle pair such as an electron  and positron or a quark and antiquark  we produce antimatter all the time in  laboratories and even in hospitals  a pet scan which stands for positron  emission tomography uses antimatter to  scan for activity in different regions  of the brain  but once we produce antimatter it only  stays around for a fraction of a second  before finding its matter counterpart  and annihilating  when the universe was initially filled  with a dense collection of elementary  particles those particles included  almost identical amounts of matter and  antimatter at the same time the quarks  combined into protons and neutrons  antiquarks combined into antiprotons and  anti-neutrons  so after a hundred thousandth of a  second the universe contained protons  antiprotons neutrons anti-neutrons  electrons and positrons  of course the matter and antimatter were  constantly annihilating creating high  energy radiation  but at the same time that intense  radiation was constantly creating new  matter and antimatter  we say that the annihilation and  creation processes were in equilibrium  meaning the rate at which matter and  antimatter was destroyed was equal to  the rate at which it was created so the  amounts of matter and antimatter  remained constant  but everything in the early universe was  subject to the effects of expansion and  cooling  as the temperature decreased the  radiation became less intense  and creation of matter antimatter  particle pairs became less likely the  equilibrium was broken and annihilations  began to outpace creation events  this happened first with the protons and  neutrons because they're heavier and  thus harder to produce than electrons  and positrons  after roughly a tenth of a second all of  the anti-protons and anti-neutrons were  gone  the positrons and electrons remained in  equilibrium with equal rates of creation  and destruction for most of the first  second  by one second however annihilation had  begun to overtake creation even for the  positrons and electrons and by three to  ten seconds later the positrons were  gone  from that moment on the observable  universe has had matter but essentially  no antimatter  after all the annihilation was done why  was there still matter  because before the annihilation i'm  describing the early universe had  slightly more matter than antimatter for  every billion antiquarks and positrons  there were a billion and one quarks and  electrons  by a few seconds after the big bang all  the antiquarks and positrons were gone  and their energy had been converted to  radiation  most of the original matter was gone too  annihilated in the collisions that  eliminated all the antimatter  but there were still those one in a  billion particles of matter left over  with nothing to annihilate  all the matter in the universe today the  stars galaxies planets and so on is made  of the one part in a billion of matter  particles left over after the antimatter  was gone  we don't know why the early universe had  a bit more matter than antimatter but  it's a very good thing for us that it  did  so by this point in our story the  antimatter was gone and a little matter  remained but there was also something  else in the universe electromagnetic  radiation which includes light x-rays  radio waves and a variety of other forms  after matter and antimatter annihilated  there was a lot of this radiation  because that's where all the energy from  those annihilations went  in the 10 second old universe radiation  had more than a billion times as much  energy as the matter  in fact the reason we know the ratio of  matter to antimatter prior to  annihilation is that we can observe the  radiation that got produced  we think of light and other forms of  electromagnetic radiation as continuous  but when you measure it with very  sensitive instruments you find that a  beam of light is made of discrete  particles of energy that we call photons  when a matter and antimatter particle  annihilate they produce two photons  in the universe today there are over two  billion photons for every electron  that tells us that for each electron in  the universe today we originally had a  billion and one electrons and a billion  positrons and when they annihilated we  were left with one electron and two  billion photons  but let's return to the matter in the  early universe  in particular i want to talk now about  neutrinos those particles that fill the  universe today but pass through solid  matter with almost no interactions  to give you a sense of how elusive these  particles are if a beam of neutrinos hit  a block of lead a light year thick  that's about 6 trillion miles  roughly half of the neutrinos would pass  through unaffected  but for the first second after the big  bang the universe was so dense that even  neutrinos couldn't pass through  neutrinos constantly collided with other  particles  starting about one second after the big  bang though the matter wasn't dense  enough to stop neutrinos anymore the  neutrinos from that early time have been  moving freely through the universe ever  since  we say that at one second the neutrinos  froze out that term doesn't mean that  their temperature changed at that moment  it means they almost entirely stopped  interacting with other matter  another term you'll hear is that the  neutrinos decoupled one second after the  big bang  since that moment those neutrinos have  been flying freely through the universe  although they haven't been interacting  with matter the neutrinos have slowed  down as a result of the expansion of the  universe  those neutrinos left over from the first  second of the universe still fill all of  space today  calculations showed that these  primordial neutrinos should be coming to  us equally from all directions at a  temperature of about two degrees above  absolute zero  in 2015 for the first time scientists  detected this cosmic neutrino background  and measured its temperature at 1.96  degrees above absolute zero beautifully  confirming our understanding of what was  happening in the first second of the  universe  the next important events had to do with  protons and neutrons  protons and neutrons can transform into  each other in a process called beta  decay  when quarks first combined they produced  equal numbers of protons and neutrons  and for a while they each transformed  into each other at equal rates  but as the temperature dropped it became  more likely for the slightly heavier  neutrons to decay into the slightly  lighter protons than the other way  around  by about one second the decay of protons  into neutrons had stopped but the other  way around neutrons turning into protons  was still happening  so the ratio of protons to neutrons was  steadily growing  if nothing had happened to preserve the  neutrons they would all have been gone  10 to 20 minutes later  but another important event did  intervene  about a minute after the big bang  protons and neutrons began combining  into light nuclei in the process we call  big bang nucleosynthesis or just  nucleosynthesis for short  the key to nucleosynthesis is that  protons and neutrons all attract each  other via the strong force  i said that when quarks combine into  groups of red green blue like protons or  neutrons the strong forces between two  of those groups mostly cancel out  but suppose two neutrons get very close  to each other  some of the quarks in each proton are  closer to the other proton than other  ones so the forces between the quarks  don't perfectly cancel  the result is that there's still a  residual strong force between the two  the same logic holds for two neutrons or  for a neutron and a proton  all of those particles attract each  other with a strong force that is much  weaker than the force between individual  quarks because those quark unquark  forces mostly but don't perfectly cancel  out  but that residual strong force between  protons and neutrons is still strong  enough to hold nuclei together  but as we saw with individual quarks the  attraction between protons and neutrons  can't hold them together if they're  moving with too much energy  for protons and neutrons to stick  together the temperature had to drop  below about a billion degrees which  happened about a minute after the big  bang  once the temperature was low enough one  proton and one neutron could combine to  form a nucleus called hydrogen two also  called deuterium  two deuterium nuclei could then combine  into helium four two protons plus two  neutrons  and for the most part that was the end  of nucleosynthesis  you can imagine combining helium four  with a single proton or neutron or a  deuterium or another helium four but  none of those happens to produce a  stable nucleus so for the most part  helium-4 is the end of the chain  if nucleosynthesis had started out with  equal numbers of protons and neutrons  for example if it had started at a tenth  of a second instead of at one minute  then essentially all the nuclei would  have ended up as helium-4  conversely if there had been no neutrons  say if nucleosynthesis couldn't start  until 20 minutes then no nucleosynthesis  would have happened at all and all  nuclei would have ended up as hydrogen  but when nucleosynthesis started at one  minute there were about seven protons  for each neutron  so each set of 14 protons and two  neutrons produced one helium nucleus and  left 12 bare protons which are hydrogen  nuclei  after nucleosynthesis helium thus made  up about 8  of the nuclei in the universe  because helium nuclei are heavier than  hydrogen nuclei that meant helium made  up about 25 percent of the nuclei by  mass  i've discussed the fact that  nucleosynthesis didn't start before the  one minute mark because the universe was  too hot for nuclear fusion  nucleosynthesis stopped a few minutes  later because the universe became too  cold  at that point there was still some  deuterium left that had not fused into  heavier nuclei and since nucleosynthesis  was no longer possible that deuterium  just stayed deuterium  but after nuclear synthesis deuterium  accounted for only about one in a  billion nuclei  nonetheless measurement of that trace  deuterium in exactly the amounts  predicted by the big bang model has been  one of the great experimental  confirmations of the model  but why did nucleosynthesis stop when  the universe got too cold  the reason is that in order for two  deuterium nuclei to fuse they have to  first get very close to each other  when you put two protons near each other  they interact in two very different ways  they attract each other through the  strong force at the same time they repel  each other through the electric force  since they're both positively charged  so which force wins  the answer is it depends on how close  the two protons are if the protons are  very very close the strong force wins  they attract much more than they repel  and that's why a nucleus can stick  together  but the strong force is a short-range  force much beyond the diameter of an  atomic nucleus about a millionth of a  billionth of a meter the strong force  pretty much drops to zero and you're  left with two protons that repel each  other electrically  so now picture two deuterium nuclei each  made of one proton and one neutron  once again they will be attracted by the  strong force and repelled by the  electric force  if they get incredibly close within a  couple nuclear diameters the strong  force will take over and they'll fuse  but how can they get that close when at  larger distances they're pushing each  other away  the answer is that they have to be  moving so fast that they plow right  through the electric repulsion of their  protons  those kinds of speeds happen at very  high temperatures  so here's the bottom line there's a  relatively narrow range of temperatures  at which two deuterium nuclei can hit  each other so hard that they get close  enough to fuse  but not so hard that they then bounce  right off again the universe was in that  temperature range from about one minute  to about three minutes  after three minutes deuterium fusion  into helium slowed considerably and it  was all over by 10 to 20 minutes later  at that point the mix of nuclei in the  universe was fixed for millions of years  until fusion started up again in the  cores of the first stars  so to sum up the story so far at a  hundred billionth of a second old our  universe was a dense collection of  quarks electrons and many other  elementary particles with nearly equal  amounts of matter and antimatter all at  a quadrillion degrees  a second later the quarks it all  combined the unstable particles like  higgs bosons had all decayed and the  antimatter had annihilated with almost  all of the matter  a minute later protons and neutrons  combine to make nuclei almost entirely  hydrogen and helium a hydrogen nucleus  is a proton that hasn't combined with  anything else and a helium nucleus is  two protons and two neutrons  in the next lecture we'll take the  history of the universe up through the  formation of stars and galaxies but  before we get stars we'll first have to  make our nuclei and electrons into atoms
 

